Porous materials like zeolites and metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) have shown great promise for gas storage and separation processes.11−13 This is largely attributed to the ability to tune their structure, composed of metallic ions or clusters and organic linkers, which leads to an infinite number of possible structures. Due to these features, MOFs can exhibit a high selectivity toward a desired target gas. MOFs have been used as active sensing materials to detect vapors and VOCs,14−20 water vapor,21,22 $\mathrm{{\bar{C}O_{2}},}^{10,23,24}\mathrm{{\mathrm{~O}_{2}},}^{25}$ and sulfur-containing gases26,27 ( $\mathrm{\DeltaSO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S\bar{}$ ). A priori, MOFs are suitable candidates for active sensing materials owing to their tunable selectivity toward a target gas, ability to be coated onto miniature sensors, and ability to function under both room and elevated temperatures. Yet, the focus of research so far has been on either sensing of or increasing the selectivity for a particular gas. Quantification of a wide range of gases has not been proven. The integration of MOF into actual sensor devices is even less explored. In short, although very promising, the development of MOF-based portable gas sensors is at its infancy and several technical challenges related to their long-term usability and implementation remain to be overcome.
Gravimetric sensors provide an excellent platform for gas sensing due to their simple miniature construction, commercial availability, low cost, and ability to provide online data.9,28,29 In these sensors, the gas uptake, on an active sensing surface (porous materials, e.g., MOFs), is inferred by changes in physical quantities like resonance frequency (for QCM) or modulation of surface acoustic waves (for SAW). The mass detection limit of these devices is usually in the order of nanograms.5,6,9 As explained previously, MOFs show great potential for gas sensing applications and can be used in a gravimetric sensor.30 Yet, a single magic MOF material can hardly ever be used to detect multiple gases in a gas stream. An array of sensors (also known as electronic nose, analogous to the human olfactory system) has been proposed to overcome the issue of multigas sensing.16,31−36 An array of sensors is composed of multiple sensor elements coated with different materials exhibiting varying selectivity toward a particular gas. There exists only a handful of experimental studies of such an array using MOFs.16,37
Given the large number of experimental and hypothetical MOFs reported in the literature, there is a necessity to select materials from the thousands of possible candidates in a smart fashion that guarantees excellent sensing performance in a sensor array. To this aim, Wilmer and co-workers and Simon and co-workers have recently provided excellent screening frameworks.38−44 The former comprises computational studies aimed at providing a probabilistic metric to quantify sensing performance of an array and at efficiently screening several materials using an evolutionary optimization algorithm for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensing. The latter comprises computational studies aimed at using elegant mathematical concepts to screen materials under dilute conditions for a two- and three-gas system and to evaluate the fitness of combinations of materials in a sensor array for a two-gas system.
The overarching goal of this work is twofold. We aim to systematically highlight the impact of thermodynamics of the gas−material system on gas sensing and to provide a simple graphical approach to screen porous materials that can be used in a gravimetric sensor. This aspect would be an addition to what was covered in the aforementioned computational studies. Once the equilibrium characteristics of a material are available, the graphical approach would provide a quick check to determine whether the material will be a suitable candidate for a given gas sensing application. If the material is not a suitable candidate, the same approach can be used to mix and match different materials to get a good sensing accuracy. Given the sheer number of MOFs reported in the literature, a simple tool like the one proposed in this work can be handy. This approach relies only on the thermodynamics of the system and is aimed to be qualitative.
For a more rigorous approach, the computational studies reported in the literature can be used.39,42 The second goal of our study is to systematically highlight the importance of incorporating adsorption kinetics for gravimetric sensors in the screening process. The computational studies reported in the literature were all performed under equilibrium conditions. However, adsorption is driven by both equilibrium and kinetics. Within the context of gas sensing, the amount of gas adsorbed will determine the changes in the sensor response and the kinetics will determine the sensor response time. The kinetic aspects of gas sensing have often been overlooked and not been studied in detail. Our work does not pertain to any specific mixture(s) of gas. The methodology, results, and conclusions from this study can be easily transferred to any gravimetric sensor with porous materials as active sensing surfaces.
## METHODOLOGY
The studies presented in this work are purely computational in nature. A simple schematic of the problem statement and the workflow of the computational test bench developed in this work are shown in Figure 1. This test bench is composed of two components, namely, a sensor array simulator and a composition estimator. The former serves as a proxy to an experimental sensor array and provides what we refer to as the “true” (or experimental) sensor response. In this work, the sensor response is equivalent to the amount of gas adsorbed on the active sensing surface (i.e., the porous material). Upon the availability of the amount of gas adsorbed, the composition estimator aims to provide the composition of the gas that was exposed to the porous material that led to the given sensor response. The accuracy of the estimated gas composition however relies on a number of factors that are discussed in detail in this article. The sensor array simulator and the composition estimator are two components of the computational test bench, which are discussed in the following subsections.
Sensor Array Simulator. To simulate the sensor array, it is necessary to first identify the gases that will be sensed and the materials that will form the array. Subsequently, the thermodynamics (i.e., adsorption equilibria) and the kinetics of the gasmaterial combination must be defined. If the kinetics is not considered, the sensor response can be obtained by estimating the gas uptake in a given material at a given partial pressure (i.e., composition). If kinetics is considered, the thermodynamics of the system is coupled with the kinetics. This is done by integrating the mass balance equations for the gas and the solid phases that define the evolution of the gas uptake of the sensor array as function of time. The following subsections provide details regarding how these cases are handled in the computational framework.
Definition of the Gas−Material System. To keep the study as general as possible, we consider hypothetical materials instead of real materials. The adsorption equilibria of these materials are defined using the single-site Langmuir isotherm (a type I isotherm). The parameters for this model are randomly generated from a bounded domain representative of the adsorption capacities and heats of adsorption of common porous materials (e.g., MOFs, zeolites). Further, the competition between the gases is defined using the extended single-site Langmuir isotherm, retaining the parameters of the pure gases, 45 as follows:
The molecular weights of the gases used in this work are $\mathbf{M}\left[\mathbf{g}\right.$ $\mathrm{mol^{-1}}\big]=\big[44.0128.\dot{0}1\big]$ . These specific molecular weights are chosen to mimic real gases like $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and CO or ${\mathrm{N}}_{2},$ respectively. However, these can be replaced with that of any gas of interest. All the simulations are performed at a temperature of 298.15 K and a pressure of 1 bar. Depending on the system being studied, the molecular weights, temperature, and pressure can be changed in the computational framework.
Sensor Response: Accounting for Only Thermodynamics. In the absence of kinetics or estimating the gas composition under equilibrium conditions, the response $m_{i}$ of a gravimetric sensor (e.g., QCM) coated with material i at pressure $P$ and temperature $T$ exposed to a gas mixture of composition y is given as
It would be unreasonable to assume that in a real experimental setting, the sensor measurement is uncorrupted by noise. Therefore, to mimic this behavior, a noise $\epsilon_{\upsilon}$ sampled from a normal distribution, is added to the sensor response. The presence/absence of noise will be explicitly mentioned in the studies discussed in the following sections
$$
\epsilon_{i}\sim{\cal N}(0,\ 0.01)
$$
Sensor Response: Accounting for Both Thermodynamics and Kinetics. When accounting for kinetics, a detailed mathematical model that incorporates the resistance offered by a porous material to adsorb a given gas must be considered. This mathematical model must describe the evolution of the mass uptake over time by coupling the thermodynamics and kinetics of the porous material. The adsorption capacity and the kinetics of the gas on a given material constitute the so-called material constraints. In practice, if one uses a QCM or a SAW sensor, additional constraints exist, for example, the maximum allowable flow rate of the gas to be sensed, the mass/volume of the active sensing material coated on the sensor, and the unavoidable dead volume of the device. These factors constitute the so-called engineering constraints.
Based on the above considerations, we formulate a mathematical model for the sensor coated with a given material. We consider that the sensor is placed inside a chamber of a given volume, and a simple schematic of this setup is shown in Figure 1b. To this aim, we assume the combined sensor and the material system to be a perfectly mixed cell. This is analogous to a packed bed in chromatography/adsorption when the length of the column tends to 0.46 Therefore, the component mass balance for gas $j$ is written as
$$
F^{\mathrm{in}}c_{j}^{\mathrm{in}}-F c_{j}=V_{\mathrm{g}}\frac{\mathrm{d}c_{j}}{\mathrm{d}t}+V_{s}\frac{\mathrm{d}q_{j}}{\mathrm{d}t}
$$
where $F^{\mathrm{in}}[\mathbf{m}^{3}\mathbf{s}^{-1}]$ and $F[\mathbf{m}^{3}\mathbf{s}^{-1}]$ are the volumetric flow rates of the gas mixture at the inlet and the outlet, respectively; $c_{j}^{\mathrm{in}}$ and $c_{j}$ are the concentrations of gas $j$ at the inlet and outlet, respectively; $q_{j}$ is the amount of gas adsorbed in the porous material; $V_{\mathrm{g}}[\mathrm{m}^{3}]$ is the dead volume of the device; and $V_{\mathrm{s}}\left[\mathrm{m}^{3}\right]$ is the volume of the porous material coated on the sensor.
We assume the rate of uptake of gas j in the porous material to be described using the linear driving force model as
$$
\frac{\mathrm{d}q_{j}}{\mathrm{d}t}=k_{j}(q_{j}^{*}-q_{j})
$$
Finally, to close the system of equations, we impose a mass conservation constraint using
$$
\sum_{j=1}^{g}y_{j}=1
$$
Additionally, if the pressure drop in the sensor is assumed to be negligible, there will be $2g+1$ unknowns, namely, the $g$ gas compositions $\mathbf{\tau}(\mathbf{y})$ , the $g$ solid-phase uptakes $\mathbf{\tau}(\mathbf{q})$ , and the volumetric flow rate at the outlet $F.$ Equations 4 through 6 provide $2g+1$ equations to estimate the $2g+1$ unknowns.
Under the assumption of negligible pressure drop and using the equation of state given in eqs 1 and 4 through 6 are reformulated as follows
$$
F^{\mathrm{in}}{\frac{P}{R T}}-F{\frac{P}{R T}}=V_{\mathrm{s}}\sum_{j=1}^{g}{\frac{\mathrm{d}q_{j}}{\mathrm{d}t}}
$$
$$
\frac{\mathrm{d}y_{j}}{\mathrm{d}t}=\frac{R T}{P V_{\mathrm{g}}}\left[F^{\mathrm{in}}\frac{P y_{j}^{\mathrm{in}}}{R T}-F\frac{P y_{j}}{R T}-V_{s}\frac{\mathrm{d}q_{j}}{\mathrm{d}t}\right]
$$
We use a stiff solver in the solve_ivp function of the scipy package in Python 3.8.5.47 to integrate the aforementioned set of equations for time span $t_{\mathrm{int}}$ [s]. These equations have to be solved for each material that is present in the sensor array. The initial conditions for the aforementioned problem are
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{y_{j}(0)=0\qquadj\in[1,g)}\\ &{q_{j}(0)=0\qquadj\in[1,g]}\\ &{F(0)=F^{\mathrm{in}}}\end{array}
$$
When performing simulations considering the kinetics of the system, apart from the gases of interest, an additional nonadsorbing inert gas is introduced. In reality, this will translate to purging the material with an inert gas to prepare the material to sense the incoming feed gas.
Composition Estimator. The estimation of gas composition with the knowledge of the amount adsorbed is the inverse problem to the traditional gas adsorption problem encountered in the porous materials/adsorption process community, that is, finding the gas uptake in a material when exposed to a gas mixture of known concentrations. One can compute the gas composition if the uptake is known, and there exists a unique solution either by employing a look-up table or by framing an optimization problem. In this work, we use the latter approach. An optimization problem is framed that minimizes the error between the “true” measurement $m_{\mathrm{exp},i}$ and the simulated measurement $m_{\mathrm{sim},i}$ obtained by varying the gas composition y. For a sensor array, the error is the sum of the individual contributions from all the materials of the array.
Mathematically, when accounting only for the thermodynamics, the optimization problem takes the following form
subject t
$$
\begin{array}{r l}{\displaystyle{\mathrm{~\boldmath~\gamma~}}0\leq y_{j}\leq1\quad}&{{}j\in[1,g]}\\ {\displaystyle{\sum_{j=1}^{g}}\gamma_{j}=1}\end{array}
$$
where $m_{\mathrm{exp}}$ and $m_{\mathrm{sim}}$ are obtained using either eq 2 or 3a and 3b.
When accounting for both thermodynamics and kinetics, the aforementioned mathematical expression has to be reformulated to incorporate the evolution of the gas uptake. Upon reformulation, the optimization problem takes the following form:
$$
\underset{\mathbf{y}}{\mathrm{minimize}}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(\frac{m_{\mathrm{exp},i}(t_{k})-m_{\mathrm{sim},i}(t_{k},\ \mathbf{y})}{m_{\mathrm{exp},i}(t_{k})}\right)^{2}
$$
subject to $0\le y_{j}(t_{k})\le1\qquadj\in[1,g]$
$$
\sum_{j=1}^{g}y_{j}(t_{k})=1
$$
where $m$ is the gas uptake on a material $i$ at time instant $t_{k}$ obtained by solving the full model given by eq 7. This expression is equivalent to minimizing the error between the “true” gas uptake profile and the simulated gas uptake profile.
We use the basinhopping algorithm of the scipy package in Python 3.8.5.47 to solve the optimization problem. The basin hopping algorithm combines a global stepping algorithm and a local minimization algorithm to ensure that the entire decision variable space (y) is explored. We use a total of 50 iterations in the basin hopping algorithm, and the minimum value obtained at the end of these iterations is assumed to be the global minimum.
## THERMODYNAMIC CONSIDERATIONS
In this section, we discuss the thermodynamic considerations, that is, the gas uptake in a given material at equilibrium, within the context of gas sensing. Subsequently, we propose a simple graphical method used to provide a quick check on whether a given material or a combination of materials has the potential to resolve accurately the composition of gases being sensed. To this aim, we provide first a simple study that highlights the need to incorporate a combination of materials for good sensing performance. Second, we show that even with a combination of materials, there is no guarantee that an optimal sensing performance can be obtained without introducing mathematical constraints to estimate the gas composition. Third, we demonstrate that even after incorporating the constraint, the ability of the material(s) to resolve the “true” gas composition accurately depends on the shape of the response curve (i.e., total gas uptake vs partial pressure of the gas). Fourth, we show that in the presence of measurement noise, not just the shape of the sensor response but also the absolute amount of gas adsorbed on the material(s) dictates the ability to accurately resolve the gas composition. Finally, we propose a simple graphical approach to provide a qualitative region of gas composition where a given material or combination of materials can exhibit good sensing performance. This work paves the way for a rapid screening of materials for gas sensing. The readers are directed to other works reported in the literature for a more quantitative approach toward material screening39,40 and for a mathematical understanding of why a combination of materials exhibits a given performance.42−44
Need for a Combination of Materials. To highlight the need to combine materials with distinct equilibrium characteristics, we conducted a simple case study with 100 hypothetical materials. Subsequently, a sensor array composed of either one or two materials was exposed to a binary test gaseous stream of a known composition $\mathbf{y}~=~\left[0.05,\ 0.95\right]$ . Based on this known composition, the “experimental” sensor response $m_{\mathrm{exp}}$ was computed using eq 2 for each material in the array. Given this sensor response, the optimization problem set in eq 9 was solved to obtain the estimated composition ŷ. This approach serves as a proxy to a real experiment using the array. If the materials in a given sensor array are capable of resolving the gas and if the numerical techniques used are robust, then $\hat{\mathbf{y}}=\mathbf{y}$ . We considered two different scenarios, one in which the mass conservation constraint, given in eq 9, was not imposed and one in which it was imposed. Ideally, the former should be sufficient if the materials exhibit good sensitivity toward changes in gas-phase composition. For example, for a two-gas system, two materials should be sufficient to resolve the gas composition accurately. If not, the constraint posed in the latter case would aid in the computation of the gas compositions by providing an additional physically consistent information on the system. The one- and two-material sensor arrays led to 100 and 4950 unique combinations, respectively, using the 100 hypothetical materials. The composition estimates from these arrays are visualized in Figure 2 in the form of a probability distribution f. We draw two main observations from the results. First, as expected, moving from a sensor array composed of one material to two materials leads to a better resolution of the gas composition as evident by the narrow distribution of compositions around the “true” gas composition. This result highlights the strength of building a sensor array such that the number of materials is at least equal to the number of gases being sensed. Second, the incorporation of the mass conservation constraint (see eq 9) in the optimization routine also leads to a better resolution of the gas composition, even for the sensor array composed of only one material due to the reasons explained above. We exploit this finding in all the studies shown in the following sections by incorporating the mass conservation constraint.
Effect of Shape of Sensor Response. When a sensor array is used in practice, at each time instant, the mass change due to gas adsorption on each material is monitored and the optimization problem defined in eq 9 is solved to obtain the gas compositions. If this operation is repeated a sufficient number of times, one can obtain a distribution of gas compositions. This distribution will be characterized by its mean $\hat{\mu}$ and standard deviation $\hat{\sigma}_{\cdot}$ . For a good sensor array, the mean should be as close as possible to the “true” gas composition and the standard deviation should be as small as possible. The former guarantees obtaining the correct gas composition, while the latter guarantees a good sensitivity of the array when subjected to changes in the feed gas composition. In practice, these two quantities depend on the sensor response of the array.
Under strictly equilibrium conditions, the response of a given sensor or sensor array, given by eq 2 (absence of measurement noise) or eqs 3a and 3b (presence of measurement noise), is a consequence of the adsorption isotherm of a gas−material combination. To highlight the effect of the shape of the sensor response as a function of the gas composition which derives from the specific gas adsorption patterns, we constructed three hypothetical arrays, each with only one material (A−C). Subsequently, these arrays were exposed to eight different “true” gas compositions (a two-gas mixture), incorporating measurement noise. Their composition estimation was repeated a 1000 times to obtain the distribution of gas compositions. The sensor performance was gauged using two metrics, namely, the relative error of the estimated mean gas composition $\psi$ and the coefficient of variation $\chi.$ . The former is defined as the relative error of mean $\hat{\mu}$ of the estimated distribution with respect to the mean of the “true” gas composition $\mu$ . The latter is defined as the ratio of the standard deviation $\hat{\boldsymbol{\sigma}}$ to the mean $\hat{\mu}$ of the estimated distribution.
The equilibrium sensor response $m$ for the three hypothetical sensor arrays as a function of the gas composition $y_{1}$ is illustrated in Figure 3a. The three arrays exhibit different adsorption behaviors. For arrays A and $\mathrm{c},$ gas 1 is preferentially adsorbed over gas 2, while for array B, gas 2 is preferentially adsorbed over gas 1. In other words, we observe differences in the shape of the sensor response. Additionally, the sensor response for arrays A and B are extremely nonlinear (in a qualitative sense) with respect to the gas composition, while for array C, it is closer to being linear. As discussed below, the shape of the response has major implications on estimating the composition accurately. The measurement noise is assumed to be normally distributed with a standard deviation of $0.1\mathrm{g}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ . Therefore, if an array has a lower absolute sensor response, the signal-to-noise ratio will be lower.
The relative error of the estimated mean gas composition ψ and the coefficient of variation $\chi$ for the three arrays at different gas compositions are shown in Figure $^{3\mathrm{b},\mathrm{c},}$ respectively. Two observations can be made based on the outcome of the simulations. First, array C performs the best over a wide range of gas compositions, as indicated by the lower value of ψ and $\chi$ over almost the entire range of compositions. At lower compositions, due to the lower signal-to-noise ratio which can be attributed to the absolute sensor response and the linear shape of the response, the accuracy of array C is impacted. Second, arrays A and B exhibit contrasting behaviors in line with the shape of their response. Array A shows higher nonlinearity at lower compositions and therefore has a lower value of $\psi$ and $\chi$ at lower gas compositions, while array B has a lower value for both the quantities at higher gas compositions. For array A, when $y_{1}>$ 0.10, and for array B, when $y_{1}<0.75$ , the sensor response barely changes, thereby leading to an incorrect mean value and a broader distribution of compositions around the mean. In the next subsection, hypothetical arrays with more than one material will be constructed to mitigate the high errors and further reinforce the observations seen here.
Evaluating Array Performance Using the Sensor Response Shape. In the previous subsections, we show that the accuracy in predicting the actual gas composition with little deviation from the mean value depends heavily on the shape of the sensor (material) response. Additionally, a nonlinear sensor response leads to a better accuracy to resolve a gas mixture whose composition falls in the nonlinear region. Based on these two observations, we developed a simple graphical tool to identify regions of gas composition to which a given sensor array will have the best performance in terms of resolving the gas mixture. To this aim, the knee/elbow of the sensor response is found using an algorithm previously reported (Kneedle algorithm).48 Here, the knee/elbow of the sensor response corresponds to the gas composition where the transition from a linear or nonlinear response to a saturated response or vice versa occurs. This approach is qualitative and aims to rapidly eliminate materials or a combination of materials that cannot resolve a gas mixture at a given composition. This method can also serve as a prescreening step to reduce the number of materials that must be screened rigorously for constructing a sensor array (e.g., using the optimization methodology proposed previously40).
Two-Material Array. To illustrate the performance of the proposed methodology, we constructed two hypothetical arrays, D and E, using two materials each ( $\alpha$ through $\delta$ ). The sensor responses for these two arrays are shown in Figure $^{4\mathsf{a},\mathsf{c}}$ . Using the Kneedle algorithm, the corresponding knee/elbow was found. The algorithm provides the value of the gas composition where the transition in the sensor response occurs. Using this tool, we identified two regions for each material, namely, a more sensitive region and a less sensitive region. The former will encompass gas compositions that a material could resolve more accurately than the latter. The sensitive region for each material is highlighted as the shaded region (orange or green) in Figure $\scriptstyle4\mathbf{a},\mathbf{c}.$ For array D, exposure to a gas mixture with $y_{1}$ below 0.4 and for array E, exposure to a gas mixture with either $y_{1}$ below 0.1 or above 0.85 should yield a better performance when compared to other gas compositions.
Further, we used the approach from the previous subsection to quantify the relative error of the estimated mean composition $\psi$ and the coefficient of variation $\chi$ for the two hypothetical arrays in the presence of measurement noise. These two quantities for arrays D and E at different gas compositions are shown in Figure $^{4\mathrm{b,d}},$ , respectively. Several observations can be made on the performance of both the arrays. Over a broad range of gas composition, array D exhibits a negligible change in performance indicated by a near constant value of $\psi$ and $\chi$ . For array D, the addition of material $\beta$ to $\alpha$ (array C) deteriorates the performance of the array due to its lower absolute sensor response, leading to a further decrease in the signal-to-noise ratio, when compared to array C (see Figure 3), over the entire composition range. At lower gas compositions, due to the linear nature of the sensor response and therefore a lower signal-tonoise ratio, array D exhibits a poorer performance indicated by the higher values of $\psi$ and $\chi.$ The difference in performance at different gas compositions and the effectiveness of using sensitive regions to gauge the array performance are clearer for array E. The performance is in line with the gas compositions that fall under the sensitive region, that is, lower values of $\dot{\boldsymbol{\psi}}$ and χ at both lower and high gas compositions. When outside the sensitive region, these quantities are 3−4 orders of magnitude higher than the best case scenario, highlighting the inability of the array to be used under conditions that have intermediate gas compositions. Array E also benefits from a higher absolute sensor response in the sensitive region. However, at intermediate gas compositions, neither material γ nor $\delta$ exhibits any changes in sensor response, leading to a poorer performance.
As discussed above, for array D, the addition of material $\beta$ to $\alpha$ led to a deterioration of the performance due to the lower absolute sensor response of material $\beta$ . In order to conclusively show the impact of the absolute sensor response on the sensor performance, we tested the performance of two other arrays. These two arrays, referred to as array ${\bf D}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaD}_{10},$ were constructed by keeping the absolute sensor response of $\alpha$ the same but increasing the absolute sensor response of $\dot{\boldsymbol{\beta}}$ by a factor of 3 $(\beta_{3})$ for the former and 10 $\left(\beta_{10}\right)$ for the latter, when compared to array D. The equilibrium sensor response for the three hypothetical sensor arrays as a function of gas composition $y_{1}$ is illustrated in Figure 5a. We further quantified the relative error of the estimated mean composition $\psi$ and the coefficient of variation $\chi$ for the two hypothetical arrays ${\bf D}_{3}$ and $\mathbf{D}_{10}$ and compared it with that for array $\mathrm{~D~}$ in the presence of measurement noise. These two quantities at different gas compositions are shown in Figure ${\mathfrak{s b}},\mathbf{c},$ respectively. Based on the trends observed for both ψ and $\chi,$ it is evident that increasing the absolute sensor response for material $\beta$ reduces both the quantities, thereby translating to a better sensor performance. This behavior can be attributed to the fact that the sensor response for material $\beta$ is linear and has a low absolute response. Due to this, for most gas compositions, the signal-to-noise ratio is low. Upon increasing the absolute sensor response for material $\beta,$ this low signal-to-noise ratio is mitigated to a great extent over a wide range of gas compositions. In order to improve the sensing performance at low compositions, one would have to introduce a material like γ that exhibits a fairly nonlinear response. To conclude, the studies with the two material hypothetical arrays qualitatively demonstrate that an array would exhibit a good sensing performance when the materials that constitute the array exhibit a high absolute sensor response and a fairly nonlinear response.
Three-Material Array. As shown in the previous subsection, the methodology proposed to identify the sensitive regions for the materials to resolve a gas mixture can describe the qualitative trends in the sensing performance for the two-material case. Here, we extended the methodology for a three-material array for two hypothetical arrays, F and G, still dealing with a binary mixture. The aim of this study is twofold. First, we illustrate the ability of the methodology and explain the qualitative trends for a three-material array. Second, we illustrate the ability of the methodology to be used to mix and match materials to broaden the working range of the array.
For this study, we first constructed a reference two-material array using materials γ and $\zeta.$ Subsequently, we constructed the two hypothetical three-material arrays, F and G, by adding $\alpha$ and $\delta,$ , respectively, to the reference array. The sensor responses for these two arrays are shown in Figure $^{6\mathsf{a},\mathsf{c}}$ . Using the Kneedle algorithm, we identified the sensitive region for each material in the array (shaded region in Figure $_{6\mathrm{a},\mathrm{c}}$ ). The sensitive region for the reference two-material array covers the gas composition $y_{1}$ below 0.1. For array F, the sensitive region expands to $y_{1}$ of around 0.4 with the addition of material $\alpha$ . For array G, the sensitive region is concentrated on the two extremities of the gas composition by the addition of material $\delta$ .
Further, we employed the approach used in the previous subsection to quantify the relative error of the estimated mean composition ψ and the coefficient of variation $\chi$ for the reference array and the two hypothetical arrays, F and G, in the presence of measurement noise. These two quantities are shown in Figure 6b,d for different gas compositions. The outcome from the study agrees with the two-material array scenario, discussed in the previous subsection. The reference two-material array performs best at gas compositions that fall within the sensitive region $\left(y_{1}\right)$ below 0.10), indicated by the lower value of $\psi$ and $\chi$ As expected, the addition of a third material to the reference array enhances the performance by 3−4 orders of magnitude, when the composition of the test gas falls within the sensitive region. For array F, the addition of material $\alpha$ improves the performance over the entire range of gas compositions. This observation is attributed to the shape of the response for material $\alpha$ (see discussion on array D). However, at low gas compositions, as with arrays C and D, due to the absolute sensor response and linear shape of the response for material $\alpha_{\iota}$ , quantities ψ and $\chi$ are impacted. Array G performs better at both lower and higher compositions when compared to intermediate compositions (see discussion on array D). We attribute these observations to the shape of the sensor responses at the different gas compositions similar to discussions for other arrays. Depending on the application of a given sensor array based on the target gas compositions, the reference array can be expanded by adding materials that would resolve the gas and the methodology proposed here could be used to rapidly evaluate if this is feasible. As a final comment, the addition of more materials to a sensor does not automatically guarantee an increase in performance. Instead, the gain in performance relies heavily on the sensitivity at a given target gas composition provided by the additional material.
To conclude, the studies presented on the two-material and three-material arrays reinforce the observations made with a one-material array regarding the impact of the nonlinearity (or shape) of the sensor response on the array performance. Additionally, the impact of the absolute sensor response and in turn the signal-to-noise ratio on the sensing accuracy is also highlighted. The studies also demonstrate the effectiveness of our methodology to use the knee/elbow of the sensor response as a proxy for the expected array performance under equilibrium conditions. The ability of the method to mix and match additional materials to improve the performance of a poor performing array is also observed using the three-material array. Overall, the methodology shown here can satisfactorily and quickly gauge the qualitative performance of a sensor array and prescreen materials before performing a rigorous optimization. We acknowledge that the graphical approach is restricted to a two- and three-gas system as visualization in four dimensions and beyond becomes challenging. Nevertheless, the modeling framework can be easily transferred to any number of gases and materials.
## THERMODYNAMIC AND KINETIC CONSIDERATIONS
The observations from the previous section show that the thermodynamic/equilibrium characteristics of the materials in a sensor array determine its performance to accurately and consistently resolve a gas mixture. However, these studies were performed under equilibrium conditions. If gas adsorption is not instantaneous or if the sensor response is not recorded after a very long time (i.e., $t\to\infty$ ), one must incorporate the kinetics of the different gases in the material during the composition estimation and screening. The kinetics of the gas adsorption on the material will determine the response time of the sensor when subjected to a step change in gas composition. A good sensor is one that has a very short response time, that is, it should detect a change in gas composition, instantaneously.
When both thermodynamics and kinetics are incorporated, the sensing problem has a time-resolved mathematical formulation. This approach is closer to reality when compared to assuming an equilibrium sensing process. Additionally, this approach facilitates incorporating engineering variables like the size of the device (in terms of the volume) and the flow rate of the gas to be sensed, which is not possible with the equilibrium studies shown in the previous section or with the studies published previously.
In this section, we present a computational case study that highlights the need to employ a descriptive model incorporating adsorption kinetics, when the gas adsorption in a material is not instantaneous or if the sensor response is not recorded after a very long time (i.e., $t\rightarrow\infty$ ). Subsequently, we simulate several scenarios to highlight the importance of factors like kinetics, flow rate of the gas, and volume of the device on the sensing performance.
Need to Employ a Descriptive Model. Here, we exposed the hypothetical array D to a step input of a binary gas mixture with an inlet composition $y_{1}^{\mathrm{in}}$ of 0.1. The feed flow rate $F_{\mathrm{in}},$ the volume of the porous material $V_{s},$ and the dead volume of the device $V_{\mathrm{g}}$ were set to $\boldsymbol{5\times10^{-7}\mathrm{m}^{3}\mathrm{s}^{-1}}$ , $5\times10^{-7}\mathrm{m}^{3},$ and $5\times10^{-7}$ $\mathbf{m}^{3}$ , respectively. We assumed the same kinetic rate constant $k$ of $0.01~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ for all gases in all materials for the sake of simplicity. However, in practice, different gas/material combinations will have different kinetic rate constants. We chose this low kinetic rate constant, which will lead to a very slow uptake, to highlight the necessity to consider kinetic effects when screening materials for sensor arrays and to reliably estimate the composition of the gas mixture.
The full model incorporating both the equilibrium and the kinetics, given by eq 7 in the absence of measurement noise, is integrated for 1000 s to generate the “experimental” gas uptake profile $\mathbf{m}_{\mathrm{exp},i}$ for each material $i$ in the array. This gas uptake profile for the hypothetical array $\mathrm{~D~}$ is shown in Figure 7a. Here, we can make two observations. First, due to the presence of adsorption kinetics, the sensor response $m$ increases over time for both the materials. Second, after 750 and $250s_{\mathrm{,}}$ the response reaches a plateau for material $\alpha$ and $\beta,$ , respectively. This plateau corresponds to the equilibrium sensor response for both the materials. It is this equilibrium value that is used in all the screening studies reported in the literature. Depending on the kinetics of the gas in a given material, the response can plateau out sooner or later than the one shown here.
Given this sensor response, we considered two cases to estimate the binary mixture gas composition $\hat{y}_{1}$ . In the first case, we estimated the gas composition without any knowledge of the adsorption kinetics, that is, at each time instant, we assumed the gas to be at equilibrium with the porous material. Therefore, the equilibrium model, given by eq 2 in the absence of measurement noise, and the optimization problem given by eq 9 are used to estimate the gas composition. In the second case, we estimated the gas composition with the knowledge of adsorption kinetics, that is, at each time instant, we considered both equilibrium and kinetic characteristics of the gas irrespective of whether the gas is at equilibrium or not. Therefore, we used the full model given by eq 7 in the absence of measurement noise and the optimization problem given by eq 10 to estimate the gas composition. The evolution of the gas compositions estimated using these two cases is shown in Figure 7b. Two observations can be made. First, the compositions estimated from the two cases differ and converge only after $750\mathrm{s},$ in line with the plateau of the sensor response [see panel (a)]. This is because the materials are at equilibrium with the feed gas under feed conditions after $750s$ . Second, even though the sensor array is subjected to a feed gas at a constant composition, the equilibrium approach incorrectly predicts a variable feed gas composition. However, the full model, with the knowledge of both equilibrium and kinetics, resolves the composition accurately from the moment the array is exposed to the gas mixture.
To conclude, this simple case study highlights the importance of adsorption kinetics on gas sensing, an aspect that is often overlooked. Using a purely equilibrium approach for material screening and/or composition estimation is reasonable if the material reaches equilibrium rapidly. Since in practice, this might be difficult to achieve, one should either focus on using materials that exhibit minimal kinetic effects or on incorporating a detailed model for screening and/or composition estimation purposes in an array, like the one used in this work. Irrespective of the approach used, one must accurately characterize the kinetic characteristics apart from the equilibrium characteristics of a given material.
The detailed sensor model is integrated for $2000\mathrm{s}$ to generate the gas uptake profile m for the hypothetical array C. The gas uptake profile from varying the aforementioned four variables is shown in Figure 8. Several observations can be made from the outcome of this study. First, an increase in the kinetic rate constant $k$ of the gas being adsorbed decreases the time to reach the saturation uptake (see Figure 8a). Therefore, a higher value of $k$ translates to a faster sensor response time when subjected to a step change in gas composition. Second, an increase in the gas volumetric flow rate $F^{\mathrm{in}}{\overline{{}}}$ also decreases the time to reach the saturation uptake (see Figure 8b). A higher $F^{\mathrm{in}}$ ensures that the change in the sensor response is dictated purely by the kinetics of the adsorption and not by the convection of the gas. Third, an increase in the total volume of the device $V_{\mathrm{T}}$ at a constant dead voidage $\epsilon$ of the device increases the saturation uptake of the sensor (see Figure 8c). This is expected as an increase in $V_{\mathrm{T}}$ translates to a higher volume of the coated porous material $V_{s}$ and thereby to a higher gas uptake. This feature might also help increase the signal-to-noise ratio of the sensor response when materials with low uptake are used in the array. Finally, an increase in the dead voidage of the device $\epsilon$ at a constant total volume of the device $V_{\mathrm{T}}$ decreases the saturation uptake of the sensor (see Figure 8d). This is expected as an increase in $\epsilon$ translates to a lower volume of coated porous material $V_{s}$ .
To summarize, desirable characteristics for gas sensing include materials with fast kinetics (material constraint), a device that has a high total volume, and a low dead voidage additionally guaranteeing the ability to process a high flow rate of the sensing gas (engineering constraints). In practice, one cannot arbitrarily set the values for these material and engineering variables. Therefore, a rigorous optimization has to be performed accounting for realistic bounds of the variables before finalizing the design of the electronic nose. As a final comment, the observations seen here can have consequences when estimating the gas compositions of the gas to be sensed. If one incorporates a detailed sensor model, the gas composition can be estimated accurately irrespective of the choice of the variables. However, erroneous gas compositions are bound to be obtained when the composition estimation algorithm is blind to the effects of these variables, as in the case of the equilibrium model.
## CONCLUDING REMARKS
Key Outcomes. The work presented here provides a new perspective into material screening for gas sensing applications. We present the first study that aims to systemically evaluate the effect of adsorption equilibrium (thermodynamics) and kinetics of the gas−material system on the performance of a gravimetric sensor array. To this aim, we have developed two computational tools: first, a sensor model, which incorporates either only the thermodynamics or a combination of both the thermodynamics and kinetics of the gas−material system, and second, a composition estimator coupled to the sensor model to obtain the composition of the gases in the feed stream. The key outcomes from this work can be summarized as follows:
• One can exploit the shape of the sensor response as a function of gas composition to rapidly screen materials for gravimetric sensor arrays. This can serve as a prescreening tool to other screening approaches presented in the literature.   
• One should incorporate both equilibrium and kinetics to accurately estimate gas compositions in a dynamic system. One should account for the gas flow rate, size of the device, and kinetics of the gas−material system when screening materials as these variables can significantly impact the sensor response.
Finally, the modeling framework and the methodology developed in this work are universal and are not restricted to any material/gas combination. If the sensing mechanism is based on gravimetry, this framework can be used to gauge the performance of any existing or new material combinations for a sensor array. Even though the results presented in this work are for a binary system, the methodology and the outcome are transferable to more than two gases.
Way Forward. The accurate characterization of adsorption thermodynamics and kinetics of the materials has major implications not only on gas sensing using novel porous materials like MOFs but also on gas separation applications. Today, there are very few reports of multicomponent adsorption equilibrium and even fewer studies on the kinetics of adsorption. If porous materials are to be used on scale for gas sensing applications, an accurate characterization is key. In our future work, we aim to address this knowledge gap by looking into sensing common gases on prototypical porous materials and characterizing the adsorption equilibrium and kinetic properties of the gas−material combination.
